By hand, after the matrix:

  cargo test --features semantic --test all                 (2.57 GB free)  -> 561 passed. It links alone; in the matrix it links
      beside six other feature sets' artifacts.
  cargo build --examples --all-features                     (2.12 GB free)  -> `ld: write() failed, errno=28`; 0.52 GB free afterwards.
      Filtering out the harmless warnings leaves exactly `ld: write() failed, errno=28`: the disk.
  cargo build --examples --all-features -j 1                (2.68 GB free)  -> the same, on example "evalbench"; 0.22 GB free after.
      Serialising the links does not help: one link of a Bevy example with `semantic` writes more than the volume has left.
  cargo clean -p bevy_siren && rm -rf target/debug/examples/rustc*          -> 2.68 GB free
  cargo check --examples --all-features                     (2.67 GB free)  -> Finished. **Every example compiles with every
      feature**; 19 per-example `.d` files in `target/debug/examples` show all of them were checked. What the disk refuses is the
      LINK of the seven Bevy-sized example binaries, not their code.

The state of the tree is unchanged by these re-runs: the two failures are disk, and the code they would have exercised (`--features
semantic`, every example with every feature) compiles and its tests pass.
